Title: Fast model load: parallel unbuffered reads, pinned memory not locked twice, faster pinned allocation
Add-on: Windows + CUDA; other platforms keep the ordinary reader. On by default, LLAMA_FAST_READ=0 turns the reader off.
Measured on a laptop (RTX 5070 Ti 12 GB, Micron 2450 NVMe, 64 GB RAM): qwen3.6-35b-a3b (CPU experts in a 19 GB
CUDA_Host buffer, load mode mlock) ready in 10.5 s instead of 34 s, qwen3.8-27b in 5.1 s, both from the drive.
Outputs are byte-identical (moe_cache_bench hashes, both models).

- llama_fast_read (include/llama-fast-read.h, src/llama-fast-read.cpp): reads byte ranges of a file with workers
  that each own a FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING handle and read at an explicit offset (32 MiB, sector aligned), handing
  the pieces to a callback. The workers start at 4 and double (up to 8) while the throughput still rises by
  10 %. Nothing goes through the system file cache, so unloading a model frees all of its RAM.
  LLAMA_FAST_READ=0 disables it, LLAMA_FAST_READ_THREADS=N fixes the workers, LLAMA_FAST_READ_CHUNK_MB=N the
  read size (telecode's per-model Benchmark & tune sets both). Other platforms: unavailable (ordinary reader).
- load_all_data (no mmap, no --check-tensors): tensors in host buffers and in a CUDA device's own buffer are read
  with it; everything else (repack / split buffers, lazy tensors, FILE * models) keeps the old path. One line
  per file at warning level (visible at the default verbosity): GiB, seconds, MiB/s, readers x read size.
- clip (mtmd) reads the projector the same way (857 MiB: 4.1 s -> 0.33 s).
- mlock mode no longer VirtualLocks a device's host buffer type (CUDA_Host): the driver has page-locked it
  already, the second lock cost about 10 s for 19 GB and kept nothing in RAM that was not kept already.
- CUDA host buffers of 64 MiB and more on Windows: allocated, their pages touched by up to 16 threads and then
  cudaHostRegister'ed instead of cudaMallocHost (4 GiB: 0.07 s instead of 0.52 s; the 19 GB buffer 9.4 s -> 0.5 s
  together with the item above). GGML_CUDA_HOST_REGISTER_ALLOC=0 restores cudaMallocHost; a failed register
  falls back to it.

Related upstream work: ggml-org/llama.cpp#26014, #26542 (Windows unbuffered / positional reads), #18012 (Linux
direct I/O), #31825 (pinned allocation cost with MoE offload).

